<article class="env-lemma" id="0F4G">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0F4G">Lemma <span data-tag="0F4G">7.38.6</span>.</a> Let $u : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}$ be a continuous functor of sites. Let $\{ (q_ i, v_ i)\} _{i\in I}$ be a conservative family of points of $\mathcal{D}$. If each functor $u_ i = v_ i \circ u$ defines a point of $\mathcal{C}$, then $u$ defines a morphism of sites $f : \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{C}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Denote $p_ i$ the stalk functor (<a href="/tag/04EH" data-tag="04EH">7.32.1.1</a>) on $\textit{PSh}(\mathcal{C})$ corresponding to the functor $u_ i$. We have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (f^{-1}\mathcal{F})_{q_ i} = (u_ s\mathcal{F})_{q_ i} = (u_ p\mathcal{F})_{q_ i} = \mathcal{F}_{p_ i}  \]
</div>
    <p>
       The first equality since $f^{-1} = u_ s$, the second equality by Lemma <a href="/tag/00Y8" data-tag="00Y8">7.32.5</a>, and the third by Lemma <a href="/tag/0F4F" data-tag="0F4F">7.34.1</a>. Hence if $p_ i$ is a point, then pulling back by $f$ and then taking stalks at $q_ i$ is an exact functor. Since the family of points $\{ q_ i\} $ is conservative, this implies that $f^{-1}$ is an exact functor and we see that $f$ is a morphism of sites by Definition <a href="/tag/00X1" data-tag="00X1">7.14.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>